Sure! If you already have your list in JavaScript, you can use the `prioritizeAndSort` helper like this:

```js
// Example list – replace this with your real data
const myItemList = [
  { name: "Alpha", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Bravo", status: "urgent" },
  { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Delta", status: "urgent" }
];

// Call the helper
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),   // Pass the array as a JSON string
  priorityStatus: "urgent",            // Items with this status go first
  ascending: true                      // Remaining items sorted alphabetically
});

console.log(sorted);
```

The output will be:

```
[
  { name: "Bravo", status: "urgent" },
  { name: "Delta", status: "urgent" },
  { name: "Alpha", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" }
]
```

If you want to see the function in action with your own data, just replace the `myItemList` array above with your actual list.
